        <article class="article">

          <p class="article-intro">
            Selling a home in Miami involves much more than placing the property
            on the market. Pricing, presentation, marketing, buyer qualification,
            negotiations, inspections, financing and closing can all affect the
            final outcome. A clear strategy from the beginning can help you
            position your property effectively and make better decisions
            throughout the sale.
          </p>


          <div class="step">

            <div class="step-number">
              STEP 01
            </div>

            <h2>
              Understand Your Miami Real Estate Market
            </h2>

            <p>
              Miami is not one single real estate market. Buyer demand, property
              values and competition can vary significantly between neighborhoods
              and even between nearby properties.
            </p>

            <p>
              A home in Coral Gables may compete with a very different group of
              properties than a home in Doral, Pinecrest, Kendall or Miami Beach.
              Before establishing an asking price, it is important to understand
              what buyers are currently seeing in your specific area.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div class="step">

            <div class="step-number">
              STEP 02
            </div>

            <h2>
              Determine the Right Asking Price
            </h2>

            <p>
              Pricing is one of the most important decisions a seller makes.
              The goal is not simply to choose the highest possible number.
              The goal is to position the property so qualified buyers see
              enough value to schedule a showing and potentially submit an offer.
            </p>

            <p>
              A pricing analysis should consider factors such as:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Recent comparable sales</li>
              <li>Current competing listings</li>
              <li>Property size and lot size</li>
              <li>Condition and renovations</li>
              <li>Location and neighborhood</li>
              <li>Days on market of comparable properties</li>
              <li>Current buyer demand</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              Pricing too high can reduce early buyer interest, while pricing
              strategically can help the property compete more effectively.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div class="step">

            <div class="step-number">
              STEP 03
            </div>

            <h2>
              Prepare the Property for Buyers
            </h2>

            <p>
              Buyers often form an opinion within the first few minutes of
              seeing a property. Presentation therefore matters both online
              and during an in-person showing.
            </p>

            <p>
              Preparation does not necessarily mean completing a major
              renovation. Depending on the property, simple improvements can
              make a meaningful difference.
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Deep cleaning</li>
              <li>Removing unnecessary clutter</li>
              <li>Improving exterior presentation</li>
              <li>Completing obvious minor repairs</li>
              <li>Improving lighting</li>
              <li>Organizing rooms to improve visual space</li>
            </ul>

          </div>


          <div class="step">

            <div class="step-number">
              STEP 04
            </div>

            <h2>
              Market the Home Professionally
            </h2>

            <p>
              Strong real estate marketing is designed to put the property in
              front of qualified buyers and the agents who represent them.
              Professional presentation is especially important because many
              buyers begin their search online.
            </p>

            <p>
              Depending on the property and marketing strategy, exposure may
              include professional photography, online real estate platforms,
              agent networks, social media, direct outreach, buyer databases
              and open houses.
            </p>

            <p>
              The objective is not simply to generate views. It is to generate
              meaningful interest from buyers who may actually be in a position
              to purchase the property.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div class="step">

            <div class="step-number">
              STEP 05
            </div>

            <h2>
              Evaluate Offers Beyond the Purchase Price
            </h2>

            <p>
              The highest offer is not automatically the strongest offer.
              Sellers should evaluate the entire proposal before deciding
              how to respond.
            </p>

            <p>
              Important considerations may include:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Purchase price</li>
              <li>Financing terms</li>
              <li>Proof of funds or loan pre-approval</li>
              <li>Deposit amount</li>
              <li>Inspection terms</li>
              <li>Appraisal considerations</li>
              <li>Requested credits or concessions</li>
              <li>Closing date</li>
              <li>Other contingencies</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              Understanding the complete structure of an offer helps a seller
              compare both the financial value and the probability of the
              transaction successfully reaching closing.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div class="step">

            <div class="step-number">
              STEP 06
            </div>

            <h2>
              Navigate Inspections, Appraisal and Financing
            </h2>

            <p>
              An accepted contract is an important milestone, but several
              steps may remain before closing.
            </p>

            <p>
              Depending on the contract and how the buyer is purchasing the
              property, the transaction may involve inspections, an appraisal,
              lender approval, title work and other contractual requirements.
            </p>

            <p>
              Keeping track of deadlines and responding quickly when an issue
              arises can be important to maintaining the transaction.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div class="step">

            <div class="step-number">
              STEP 07
            </div>

            <h2>
              Prepare for Closing
            </h2>

            <p>
              As the transaction approaches closing, the parties work toward
              satisfying the remaining contractual and title requirements.
              Closing documents are prepared, final figures are reviewed and
              the transaction is completed according to the contract.
            </p>

            <p>
              Sellers should understand their estimated proceeds before
              closing so they have a clear picture of the financial result
              of the transaction.
            </p>

          </div>


          <h2>
            What Does It Cost to Sell a House in Miami?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cost of selling a property depends on the specific transaction.
            Potential seller expenses can include brokerage compensation,
            closing-related charges, taxes, title-related expenses, agreed
            repairs, credits or other negotiated costs.
          </p>

          <div class="info-box">

            <h3>
              Know Your Estimated Net Proceeds
            </h3>

            <p>
              Before accepting an offer, it is useful to evaluate the estimated
              amount you may receive after mortgages, liens and applicable
              transaction expenses are accounted for.
            </p>

          </div>


          <h2>
            Should You Sell Before Buying Your Next Home?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This depends on your finances, timing and individual goals.
            Some homeowners need proceeds from their current property before
            purchasing another home. Others may have the ability to purchase
            first and sell afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            When both transactions are connected, planning the timeline early
            can reduce unnecessary pressure and help coordinate the transition
            between properties.
          </p>


          <h2>
